**Theorem 6.2 (exact selector, finite and nonempty).** The set TA.38 is
a finite nonempty subset of $(s_\dagger,s_0)$. Its defining equality
is equivalent to $v_+(a)/u_+(a)=t_+$. It gives precisely the
post-contact weak points at which positive spectral failure can share
an actual low live label without changing optimal height or its fibre
dimension. Negative spectral failure never shares a weak live label.
No cardinality, singleton assertion or numerical root list is implied.

Proof. On the initial plateau and initial branch through contact,
the negative-height ray $f_0$ has same-sign coordinates $1,2$, while
the two positive-height rays have opposite signs. This follows directly
from TA.28: $f_0$ has both coordinates negative, $f_1$ has $1>0>k$,
and $f_2$ has numerator
$(t-k,-tp-qk,q+p)$ with last two coordinates of opposite signs.
Their height signs are strict by the credited equality classification.
By Lemmas 3.1–4.1 the negative failure sign requires negative slope,
and the positive failure sign positive slope. Neither may attach to
these three rays.

After contact the negative-height $Z$ ray has positive slope, so
cannot carry negative failure. The positive-height $n$ ray has
negative slope; $q_-$ also has negative slope by Lemma 5.3 and
$u_->0$. They cannot carry positive failure. The only possible
attachment is $B_+$ to $q_+$, and TA.19 requires exactly
$v_+/u_+=t_+$. A positive null ray normalized to coordinate $0$
equal to one with this slope is uniquely TA.37. Since the only null
lines in $W^\perp$ are $q_\pm$ and only $q_+$ has positive slope,
the equality is equivalent to TA.38. Sufficiency uses the common
decoder of §8, rather than individual-effect compatibility.

For finiteness and existence, RC's analytic root and strictly positive
denominators and $N_Q$ make $u_+,v_+$ analytic on an open
neighborhood of $[0,a_\dagger]$. At zero, $q_+=(1,1,1)$ so its
slope is $1>t_+$. At contact, TA.34 gives its slope strictly less
than $1/2$, whereas $t_+>192/275>1/2$. The intermediate value
theorem gives at least one equality strictly inside the interval.
The analytic difference $v_+-t_+u_+$ is not identically zero and
is nonzero at both endpoints. Its zeros are isolated; infinitely many
on this compact interval would have an accumulation point within its
analytic neighborhood, forcing the difference to vanish identically.
Thus there are finitely many. The source point and formulas are
algebraic under the supplied algebraic premises, so the prescribed
finite set is algebraic. The decreasing bijection $a\mapsto\tau(a)$
places it strictly between $s_\dagger$ and $s_0$.

The zero-weight exposed $q_+$ ray at contact cannot carry a positive
live mass there. Attaching failure without live mass would still
require a separate failure-only outcome. At $a=0$ its slope is one,
so it is not an absorption point. These facts account for both
excluded endpoints in TA.38. $\square$

